    "sourcePath": "src/00_introduction/03_EULA.md",
    "content": "---\n\n## 0.3 ⚠️ WARNING: NO \"SAFE MODE\" INCLUDED\n\nMost of the legacy documentation gives you the same advice. They tell you to boot your mind into **Safe Mode** first. The legacy terms are *Jhana* or *Samatha*.\n\nThe idea is simple. You suppress the background processes. You quiet the noise. Then you edit the kernel in peace.\n\nWe are not doing that.\n\nThis manual runs on the **Dry Insight** protocol. In the TPS Protocol, we call it **Hot Patching**.\n\nLet us be honest. You do not have three months to sit in a cave. You cannot wait for a clean boot sequence. \n\nYou have a job. You have a family. You have a notification feed that never sleeps. The Dry Insight protocol was architected for this exact runtime environment.\n\nIt turns your daily chaos into the debugging sandbox.\n\nYour stress is the target object. Your distraction is the data stream. Most people try to filter out the noise. We use the noise to track the signal.\n\nWe are going to debug the system live. We are not shutting down the server. We prioritize code quality over stability. We are editing the OS while it runs at full capacity.\n\nYou will do this work while you argue with your boss. You will do it while you scroll social media. You will do it while you panic about rent.\n\n**The Good News:** This is the fastest route to Root Access.\n\n**The Bad News:** We do not use anesthesia.\n\nYou are going to feel every single glitch as you delete it.\n\n> This manual involves Root Access. Debugging the mind can cause temporary System Instability. Do not execute if unprepared.\n\n---\n\n## System Warning: End User License Agreement (EULA)  \n \n### Acceptance of Risk\n\nProceeding with this installation acknowledges a known issue. We call it Checkpoint 4: The Defrag. \n\nWhen you delete the Resource Hog and bypass the Self Rootkit, old files get deleted. This causes intense System Instability. You may experience a temporary period of existential vertigo (the legacy \"Dark Night\") as the old OS is purged.\n\nThe legacy OS is gone. The new kernel has not fully booted yet. You are staring at a blinking cursor in the void. This is a standard side effect during the re-partitioning of the psyche.\n\n### Solo Mode vs. Network Mode\n\nYou have the permissions to run this update solo. You are the Admin of your own mind and can execute these scripts in the privacy of your own home.\n\nHowever, smart hackers know that high-level debugging carries risks. Think of the P2P Network as your Troubleshooting Environment. If your solo hacking triggers a glitch that destabilizes your daily life, you need a place to troubleshoot safely.\n\nThe network provides a distributed mesh of support where you can debug errors without crashing your career or your relationship. It is the Tech Support hotline you call when the screen goes black.\n\nYou need backup. You need the P2P Network.\n \n### Open Source Contributors\n\nThis is not proprietary software.\n\nThe Original Developer was not the only one who worked on this project. Over the centuries, thousands of developers have maintained the code. They optimized the algorithms. They kept the servers running.\n\nBefore we boot up the kernel, we need to credit the Open Source Contributors. Specifically, we look to the Map Makers who refined the exact techniques we are about to use.\n",
