    "tagline": "Finally, a word on your user permissions.",
    "sourcePath": "src/00_introduction/07_user_role.md",
    "content": "---\n\n## 0.7 The User Role: White Hat Hacker  \n  \nFinally, a word on your user permissions.\n\nIf the **Self Rootkit** is the malware, then who are you?\n\nYou are the **Observer Module**. You are the diagnostic protocol capable of watching the data stream without tagging it. You are not a separate entity inside the machine. You are simply **The Kernel** observing itself.\n  \nYou are not reading this to become a follower. You are reading this to become a **White Hat Hacker**.\n  \nA Black Hat uses system exploits for egoic gain. This corrupts the save file. A White Hat executes the protocol to prevent a total system collapse. \n\nMake your choice now. \n\nIf you try to use these tools to build a better \"Self,\" the system will overheat. The firmware will crash. If you align your intent to clear the network lag for everyone, you get Root Access. \n\nBut you cannot hack a system if you do not know how it renders. A script kiddie just runs a stolen exploit and hopes for the best. A true hacker maps the architecture first. \n\nWe are done treating the simulation like a philosophical theory. Now we are going to tear it apart. We need to audit your biological hardware before we execute the patch.\n\n**Boot sequence complete.** **System ready.** **Initiating Hardware Diagnostics.**\n",
